By PRANK sTITT.
The babe 10 lying quiet now;
Step softly TO It's cot;
le see no pain-prints on its brow;
It's restless head moves not.
A dsinty. ripping music-trill
From cberub-harp floats round;
Her very breath seems standing still,
As though to catch the sound.
A happy smile breaks DER her face
She knowcth every tone;
The music gushes from that place
Which soon shall be her own.
Her eyelids fall upon her cheek;
Her cheek is growing white;
Her sight 10 shadowy and weak,
And yet 'tis wondrous bright;
She sees what you cannot see
You who around her stand;
She sees pathway fowery,


Out in the Swallowed land.
But wake her not; 'twere better 20;
She lieth peacefully:
Her little life doth outward flow
Sweet to eternity.
The shadows round her heart unfurl,
To let her spirit roam;
The darling little baby girl


Has smilingly gone home
Smooth not the face, but let the smile
Upon her lips remain;
Be patient-in a little while


You'll greet that smile again

OUR SECRET DRAWER.
There Is q secret drawer In every heart.


wherein we lay our treasures one by one;
Each dear remembrance OF the buried past.


Each cherished record Of the time that's gone
The old delights Of childhood long ago;


The thingb we loved because we knew them best:
The hrst-dscovered primrose In our path;


The cuckoo's earliest note, the robn's nest;
T1e emerry.haymaking around our home,


our rambles In the sunmer woods and lanes;
me story told beside the winter FIRE,


While the Wind moaned across the window psues.
The golden dreams we dreampt In after years:
Those magic visions Of our young romance;
Tne sunny nooks, the fountain and the flowers,
diidins the fairy landscape Of our trance;
The links which bound Us later still, to ous
VINO nils q corner In our life to-day, p '
without whose love, we dare NOT dream how dark


Inc rest would seem II 1t were gone away;
The songs that filled our souls with very ioy,


The gentle word that unexpected came;
Tne gilt we prized because the thought was kind;
The thousand thousand things that have no name.
III these In some far.hidden corner lie,


Within the mystery Of that secret drawer
whose magic springs though stranger hands may touch
fet none may gaze upon Its guarded store
